  "intro": "Rocks cause damage. Avoid the rocks.",
  "design": {
    "focus": "Show, don't tell. Rocks are strung one behind another into weaving lines that bend across the lanes, so the open racing line keeps moving and the player must steer to follow it. No lane read is spelled out in text — the moving line teaches it. A few cones lure the player onto the open edge exactly where a rock line bulges to the far side, and the loop ends with a short, gentle thread-the-needle corridor of parallel rocks on the final straight — a low-stress capstone, sized for fun, not frustration.",
    "damageMeaning": "The intro never explains damage. The first time the player actually clips a rock — proving they need the lesson — a one-time notification explains that rocks crack the windscreen, exactly like Lesson 1 only rehearses the airbrake once a player drifts off the road. Cracks are trophy-only: they never slow, wreck, or restart the player.",
    "goldMeaning": "Tiers grade steering and damage only. Gold: zero windscreen cracks AND every edge cone collected on the finishing lap. Silver: zero cracks. Bronze: fewer than four cracks. Four cracks earns no trophy but still completes and unlocks the next lesson — progression is never blocked. Cones exist purely to prove mastery of movement and damage; they never gate anything below gold. Smashing a cone now bursts and pops in every mode, not only when it belongs to a sweep objective."
  },
